<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wörlitz Park</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm is a group of landscape parks laid out in the late 18th century around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-frederick-franz">Leopold III Friedrich Franz</a> of Anhalt-Dessau and his architect Friedrich Wilhelm von Erdmannsdorff. Inspired by English gardens, it combined parks, palaces and farmland in an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideal of beauty and usefulness. Wörlitz Park, begun in 1769, is its centrepiece. It became a World Heritage Site in 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dessau-worlitz-garden-realm.jpg" alt="Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jwaller · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W%C3%B6rlitzAmaliengrotte.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
